course: dictionary
lesson: 4487
title: Hatchet: a tool and an idiom
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: A hatchet is a small axe with a short handle. You hold it in one hand and use it to chop wood. «That evening the Man sets out his two boots, his little stone axe, a piece of wood and a hatchet.» The clue is the piece of wood: a hatchet is a tool you use on wood.
explain: Sometimes a word has a different meaning inside a phrase. «Bury the hatchet» is an idiom, so the words together mean something other than digging with a tool. «Even friends who scream horrible things at each other can make up if both say sorry and mean it» tells you that bury the hatchet means stop fighting.
explain: Ask yourself what would fit in the sentence. If someone «buries the hatchet», they do not dig a hole. They stop arguing. Swap in your meaning, "stop fighting", and check that the sentence still makes sense.
explain: A hatchet is sharp, so it is a tool for adults or for children with close adult supervision. Always ask an adult before you use one.
fact: A hatchet is a small axe with a short handle, used with one hand to chop wood.
fact: «Bury the hatchet» means to stop arguing and make peace.
fact: «A hatchet job» means harsh, unfair criticism that tries to damage someone.
fact: Hatchets are sharp tools, only for adults or used with close adult supervision.
exercise: What is a hatchet? | A large boat for crossing rivers | A soft blanket for sleeping | *A small axe with a short handle, used with one hand to chop wood | A type of bird
exercise: «Even friends who scream horrible things at each other can make up if both say sorry and mean it.» What does «bury the hatchet» mean? | Give your friend a present | *Stop fighting and make peace | Shout louder at your friend | Dig a hole in the garden
exercise: Maya says her brother's review of her play was a hatchet job, cruel and unfair. What does a hatchet job mean? | A job done with a real hatchet | A quick, tidy piece of woodwork | A kind review with lots of praise | *Harsh, unfair criticism meant to damage someone
test: Why should children not use a hatchet on their own? | Hatchets are too light to chop anything | *Hatchets are sharp, so they are for adults or used with close adult supervision | Hatchets are only for painting | Hatchets only work in summer
test: When friends «bury the hatchet», what do they do? | *Make up after a fight | Start a new argument | Buy a new axe | Hide a toy in the sand
exam: Which meaning fits: «Maya's review of the play was a hatchet job, cruel and unfair»? | A picnic basket for the park | Stop arguing and make peace | *Harsh, unfair criticism meant to damage someone | A small axe with a short handle
